WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.580
All right, back from Justice League. I'm just gonna come right out the gate here, and maybe start with...

00:00:07.580 --> 00:00:09.580
The bottom line:

00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:11.660
I did not hate it

00:00:12.580 --> 00:00:14.580
It's not good. It's not good.

00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:16.690
It's not good

00:00:16.690 --> 00:00:18.520
But I didn't hate it

00:00:18.520 --> 00:00:20.879
I feel like that's a good starting point.

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:27.820
I'm expecting that audiences are actually going to be very soft on the movie that they're gonna

00:00:27.880 --> 00:00:32.760
Be willing to forgive it a lot specifically because the

00:00:32.820 --> 00:00:36.780
the final 20 minutes of the movie are actually pretty entertaining

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:38.120
Um..

00:00:39.820 --> 00:00:41.590
Otherwise..

00:00:41.590 --> 00:00:44.520
Otherwise.. it's a mess

00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:46.580
And it does not start out

00:00:46.580 --> 00:00:53.480
on its best best foot. The first shot of the movie you may have heard---okay, you may have heard

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:57.500
that during reshoots, Henry Cavill had

00:00:57.720 --> 00:00:59.720
facial hair.

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:02.560
He had had a mustache

00:01:02.680 --> 00:01:09.140
that he grew for another movie, for another role that he was in and he was contractually prevented

00:01:09.140 --> 00:01:13.800
from shaving it and Warner Brothers was not able to negotiate between all of the producers,

00:01:13.980 --> 00:01:20.520
between all of the different producers, and so they did the reshoots with the mustache and

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:23.000
removed it digitally in post.

00:01:23.260 --> 00:01:26.060
Aaaaaand boy does it show...

00:01:26.140 --> 00:01:30.020
And the first shot of the movie, which is simulated iPhone footage,

00:01:30.340 --> 00:01:31.360
Um

00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:35.320
Is one of these shots and it is...

00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:38.120
Not. Good.

00:01:39.060 --> 00:01:45.500
So, this actually kind of helps me on on my end

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:51.720
Because it's so obvious, the parts where     [uncomfortable laughter]

00:01:51.760 --> 00:01:55.640
The parts where they have removed the moustache

00:01:55.640 --> 00:02:03.160
are so obvious that it becomes a really easy marker of, like, okay, "What did they go in and do reshoots of

00:02:03.240 --> 00:02:06.920
when Whedon was brought on board?"

00:02:07.040 --> 00:02:09.160
Aaaand, it's a lot.

00:02:09.160 --> 00:02:14.960
Now, the final edit of a movie is always going to bias towards the reshoots

00:02:15.400 --> 00:02:22.960
for kind of obvious reasons that it's like, all right, well they went back specifically to get that footage so

00:02:23.360 --> 00:02:25.440
that footage is more likely to end up in

00:02:26.140 --> 00:02:31.380
The finished movie, in the in the final cut, and in this case, like you're looking---

00:02:31.380 --> 00:02:39.520
[sigh] I would need to go through and like actually count it, but it is substantial.

00:02:39.520 --> 00:02:45.680
The finished cut is somewhere between 25%,

00:02:46.160 --> 00:02:51.880
Maybe even as high as 50% depending on what sequences

00:02:51.880 --> 00:02:55.240
were originally intended to be included, but

00:02:55.520 --> 00:03:04.560
it's substantial: at least a quarter of the movie that you will see on screen is from Whedon's reshoots.

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:11.120
And gauging that is easy,

00:03:11.120 --> 00:03:17.520
thanks to Henry Cavill's truly, truly uncanny likeness.

00:03:17.680 --> 00:03:24.920
He appears in this movie almost entirely as CGI, a lot of body double replacement, obviously a lot of face replacement.

00:03:26.120 --> 00:03:32.480
It's very unsettling and it indicates

00:03:33.560 --> 00:03:40.400
a huge course correction in terms of where where they decided to go with the movie,

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:44.760
Midstream and in production

00:03:44.760 --> 00:03:51.280
I would be fascinated to see the Zack Snyder version

00:03:54.040 --> 00:03:57.871
Because this is definitely--this is definitely not it.

00:03:57.880 --> 00:04:03.200
Like even if he has expressed his approval of it,

00:04:03.200 --> 00:04:06.440
this is not his movie at---well...

00:04:06.440 --> 00:04:09.600
..It's half his movie. The first half is definitely his movie.

00:04:10.200 --> 00:04:16.960
The most straightforward comparison to make actually is Suicide Squad.

00:04:17.120 --> 00:04:24.080
It has a lot of the same issues as--or no, I shouldn't say it has the same issues.

00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:34.760
Because it's actually not, it's the opposite of that. It doesn't have the same issues, it has the same density of

00:04:35.200 --> 00:04:36.840
problems as

00:04:36.840 --> 00:04:39.320
Suicide Squad, but in completely different ways.

00:04:40.120 --> 00:04:50.640
In fact, of the movies that have had just kind of like this baffling sort of editing that I've seen,

00:04:50.640 --> 00:04:55.880
so if you're talking Suicide Squad, The Snowman, and now Justice League,

00:04:55.880 --> 00:05:02.200
all three of them are actually very distinct in terms of like what their actual...problems are

00:05:02.200 --> 00:05:06.600
So, where Suicide Squad they went in and they took

00:05:06.600 --> 00:05:09.360
a three- or four-hour movie and

00:05:10.760 --> 00:05:14.280
Didn't just try to compress it into

00:05:14.640 --> 00:05:18.520
a digestible package, into a two-hour movie,

00:05:18.520 --> 00:05:22.640
They also had all the competing edits

00:05:22.640 --> 00:05:28.069
And they got really funky with trying to to spice up

00:05:28.560 --> 00:05:35.090
The formula as they were doing it, so you end up with with really, really jarring, incomprehensible

00:05:35.370 --> 00:05:39.886
Sort of editing in large chunks of it because they're moving so much around

00:05:39.886 --> 00:05:43.120
Like they're actually restructuring

00:05:43.400 --> 00:05:49.800
Much of the early plot of the film in order to try and make it work in a new stylized package

00:05:50.920 --> 00:05:56.960
This one is a similar starting problem

00:05:56.960 --> 00:06:00.760
in that they had a movie that was way too long,

00:06:02.160 --> 00:06:05.560
But even after the reshoots, they still---

00:06:06.320 --> 00:06:13.360
So, Whedon went and did the reshoots and then in August, they had a more or less three-hour cut

00:06:13.360 --> 00:06:15.680
that they were test screening.

00:06:15.680 --> 00:06:18.520
They were telling exhibitors basically to expect a

00:06:19.240 --> 00:06:21.360
Three-hour movie

00:06:21.360 --> 00:06:24.280
And then, the one that gets to theaters is

00:06:24.920 --> 00:06:36.280
Two hours and so almost an hour, like 50-55 minutes, disappeared and it shows.

00:06:36.760 --> 00:06:45.960
But rather than reorganizing and restructuring it, they just they sent an editor into the booth to be just absolutely ruthless

00:06:45.960 --> 00:06:53.960
and just told them, like, "No mercy. Get it down to two hours

00:06:53.960 --> 00:06:57.960
and you have two weeks to do it"

00:06:57.960 --> 00:07:01.520
And whoever it was went in and was ruthless and just cut out.

00:07:01.840 --> 00:07:10.960
They didn't get fancy, they didn't try to spice it up.

00:07:10.960 --> 00:07:17.440
They just cut out every establishing shot, every brooding moment, every ounce of padding everything that wasn't

00:07:18.440 --> 00:07:22.560
Vital to the plot or a joke.

00:07:22.560 --> 00:07:26.040
And *jokes* brings me to the next

00:07:27.360 --> 00:07:31.760
[pained] Thing to talk about which is tone.

00:07:32.720 --> 00:07:36.880
There's a distinct tone problem in the movie

00:07:38.320 --> 00:07:45.480
The two directors have very much left their own voice in the film.

00:07:47.080 --> 00:07:54.040
You can distinctly tell the stuff that is Snyder's because it looks and feels like

00:07:54.160 --> 00:07:57.000
Snyder's stuff.

00:07:57.200 --> 00:08:01.080
It looks and feels like the previous two movies.

00:08:04.480 --> 00:08:07.600
The Whedon stuff doesn't

00:08:08.200 --> 00:08:13.800
And the Whedon decision-making in the cut shows.

00:08:13.800 --> 00:08:16.680
And whether or not this is actually like *Whedon* Whedon or if this was

00:08:17.760 --> 00:08:25.369
Corporate mandate from Warner Brothers, who knows, but they have, in the cut, decided to leave in

00:08:26.400 --> 00:08:28.380
what feels like

00:08:28.380 --> 00:08:30.760
every single

00:08:30.960 --> 00:08:33.240
Barry Allen

00:08:33.600 --> 00:08:40.440
Snide remark whether or not they should have, or whether or not it actually belonged

00:08:41.800 --> 00:08:48.520
If you've seen the trailers, there's a joke that they put in the trailers, as they put rather prominent in the trailers,

00:08:48.520 --> 00:08:52.160
where Barry basically pulls

00:08:52.170 --> 00:08:53.960
Batman aside and they have

00:08:53.960 --> 00:08:55.920
Back and forth about like,

00:08:55.920 --> 00:09:00.050
"Oh man, like, it's great that you guys look like you're ready to go and do battle

00:09:00.050 --> 00:09:03.680
But I've never done battle before I've just pushed people and run away."

00:09:03.680 --> 00:09:05.990
It's like, hey you want to keep that!

00:09:06.000 --> 00:09:07.480
It's kind of a funny line.

00:09:07.480 --> 00:09:20.480
However, that line--that gag--is inserted, like sandwiched between moments of

00:09:20.680 --> 00:09:24.000
Steppenwolf just executing people.

00:09:25.480 --> 00:09:31.240
I'm not talking like you know, comic book movie executions,

00:09:31.600 --> 00:09:38.720
I'm talking bone-cracking gurgle deaths that, even if they're happening off screen,

00:09:39.280 --> 00:09:44.720
is somewhat more sinister

00:09:44.720 --> 00:09:53.640
because the sounds that telegraph these off-screen deaths are...

00:09:54.120 --> 00:09:55.740
...Gross.

00:09:55.740 --> 00:09:57.420
Not inappropriate,

00:09:57.420 --> 00:10:00.769
Just gross like the kind of tonal

00:10:01.600 --> 00:10:09.760
sound and environment building that you have come to expect from these Snyder Superman movies.

00:10:10.120 --> 00:10:16.960
And so to go from Steppenwolf basically ripping a person's head off

00:10:16.960 --> 00:10:23.200
to Barry Allen jokin' jokin' about how he's not ready to do battle

00:10:23.800 --> 00:10:33.080
back to Steppenwolf killing someone, like breaking their back and throwing them into a wall is

00:10:34.800 --> 00:10:38.200
Mm. Not great flow.

00:10:38.200 --> 00:10:43.440
Like, maybe pick one tone or the other.

00:10:43.440 --> 00:10:47.600
The climax of the movie has a completely different tone

00:10:47.920 --> 00:10:51.160
Than everything else and, to its benefit,

00:10:53.520 --> 00:10:59.160
The the dialogue doesn't get Whedon-esque ever.

00:10:59.160 --> 00:11:05.800
I mean aside from how Barry's kind of always in there, but it's not a problem.

00:11:06.780 --> 00:11:08.780
It's not.

00:11:09.160 --> 00:11:11.600
Overall, it's not not really there, but the

00:11:13.280 --> 00:11:19.120
Whedon understands why people like Superman and

00:11:20.000 --> 00:11:27.120
Whedon has--you can tell because they're reshoots because of the mustache removal--Whedon has

00:11:27.320 --> 00:11:32.720
made Superman into kind of the Superman that everybody sort of

00:11:33.360 --> 00:11:40.159
Wanted slash expected all along and so the the climax is a lot more lighthearted,

00:11:40.320 --> 00:11:45.200
It's a lot more adventuresome, it's a lot more friendly,

00:11:45.200 --> 00:11:48.000
It's less oppressive a tone

00:11:48.640 --> 00:11:51.240
People are happy?

00:11:51.240 --> 00:11:57.400
Like, they're making jokes because they're having fun not because it's gallows humor

00:11:59.370 --> 00:12:01.400
It's very weird like

00:12:01.400 --> 00:12:03.400
[laughs]

00:12:03.600 --> 00:12:13.320
Even the parts that aren't Super Obvious Mustache Removal, it's still very kind of uncanny watching

00:12:13.520 --> 00:12:15.520
Henry Cavill's Superman

00:12:16.480 --> 00:12:18.240
smile.

00:12:18.560 --> 00:12:26.200
It's its own kind of unsettling because it's like they've given him just this resting sneer

00:12:26.200 --> 00:12:28.920
for two movies now and

00:12:29.000 --> 00:12:32.880
I've spent so much time with that sneer that

00:12:33.280 --> 00:12:37.560
watching him smile is a little

00:12:38.400 --> 00:12:44.320
It's a little unsettling and a weird kind of thing that they did with this movie is their sort of whole approach to

00:12:44.970 --> 00:12:49.309
Dealing with Batman vs. Superman is just to ignore the parts of the

00:12:50.400 --> 00:12:56.509
Everything about Batman vs Superman that they just don't want to deal with.

00:12:56.960 --> 00:13:01.000
Which would not normally be a problem except for how

00:13:01.560 --> 00:13:09.000
Intrinsically tied to Batman vs. Superman it is.

00:13:09.400 --> 00:13:16.000
Like, literally everything about the plot. This movie does not make a lick of sense if you haven't seen

00:13:16.590 --> 00:13:19.249
Batman vs. Superman, it is a hard sequel.

00:13:20.750 --> 00:13:28.549
So, in that regard it's very weird to have everyone kind of behaving as

00:13:30.240 --> 00:13:35.600
Though events in Batman vs. Superman went differently.

00:13:35.600 --> 00:13:46.040
One of the main things is that the whole first act, like prologue into the first act of this movie,

00:13:46.040 --> 00:13:52.400
is predicated on the explicit

00:13:52.640 --> 00:13:58.080
Declaration that since Superman's death things have gotten worse, but

00:13:59.730 --> 00:14:07.009
There is tied to that--and this comes in heavily during the the intro credits sequence, which is a

00:14:07.600 --> 00:14:14.680
very Zack Snyder introduction.

00:14:15.160 --> 00:14:19.840
Like, you will have Watchmen flashbacks if if you watch it.

00:14:19.840 --> 00:14:28.400
The storytelling in that sequence implies that

00:14:28.480 --> 00:14:37.840
all of these things, from war and poverty and whatnot, are escalating dramatically

00:14:37.840 --> 00:14:43.960
as a direct consequence of the death of Superman. That's what it says

00:14:44.639 --> 00:14:46.639
using cinematic language.

00:14:46.840 --> 00:14:50.200
...And also regular language

00:14:50.320 --> 00:14:56.280
Because people, in slightly different words, say exactly that.

00:14:57.040 --> 00:15:05.640
The problem there is that the world-building assumption of Batman vs Superman

00:15:06.080 --> 00:15:13.040
completely disagrees with that assessment because the world-building assumption of Batman vs Superman is that the world was

00:15:13.600 --> 00:15:15.600
a crap hole

00:15:16.000 --> 00:15:23.440
And Superman could do nothing to stem the tide of it. That is core to the identity of

00:15:23.560 --> 00:15:26.200
Batman vs. Superman is

00:15:26.480 --> 00:15:30.000
Revolving around this whole

00:15:30.000 --> 00:15:32.800
hand wringing over Superman

00:15:33.480 --> 00:15:37.240
Actually making the world worse by

00:15:37.440 --> 00:15:44.040
Doing stuff like that, that he wasn't making the world better and the things that he was doing weren't

00:15:44.880 --> 00:15:49.880
Fixing anything substantial and he

00:15:50.360 --> 00:15:54.120
wasn't giving people hope, not en masse.

00:15:54.120 --> 00:15:59.160
They kept saying, "Oh, well, he's inspiring people," but by and large the world

00:15:59.600 --> 00:16:12.240
in its entirety, in the sense of The World was you know cynical and decaying and unworthy of Superman.

00:16:12.800 --> 00:16:20.720
Perry White has a whole diatribe in

00:16:20.920 --> 00:16:30.800
In Batman vs. Superman about how the world, how America's conscience died with

00:16:31.400 --> 00:16:34.760
Jack Robert and and Martin.

00:16:36.640 --> 00:16:38.040
You know that I...

00:16:38.560 --> 00:16:41.280
[prolonged cringe]

00:16:41.600 --> 00:16:45.040
...I just had somebody in my mentions a couple days ago

00:16:45.680 --> 00:16:54.480
Arguing that the the central premise of Batman vs. Superman is that

00:16:56.280 --> 00:17:03.080
The world was too cynical for him, the world didn't deserve Superman.

00:17:03.400 --> 00:17:10.240
And I mean that doesn't work in the face of the fact that, you know..

00:17:11.040 --> 00:17:13.680
Superman in that movie is an asshole

00:17:14.520 --> 00:17:21.160
Anyway, they do a very thorough job of establishing in

00:17:21.320 --> 00:17:27.280
BVS that the world sucks and things are bad, and

00:17:27.560 --> 00:17:33.480
No matter what Batman, no matter what Superman does, nothing seems to actually get better.

00:17:33.480 --> 00:17:36.560
So to go from that to,

00:17:36.560 --> 00:17:40.720
"Oh, man, things are, you know,

00:17:40.800 --> 00:17:47.520
Basically things were roses back when Superman was alive, but now everything's fallen part,"

00:17:49.880 --> 00:17:56.200
is kind of weird and more than a little strange.

00:17:58.480 --> 00:18:08.640
I suppose maybe you could view it as a bit of a spoiler-ish that Superman's in this a lot, but it's hard to avoid

00:18:09.080 --> 00:18:13.080
because he's actually in this movie a lot.

00:18:13.480 --> 00:18:19.160
The trailer kind of waffled back and forth between pretending that he was going to stay dead and

00:18:19.480 --> 00:18:21.920
just acknowledging that like

00:18:22.159 --> 00:18:24.159
Yeah, he's back

00:18:25.880 --> 00:18:30.720
'Cuz he comes back, actually they they resurrect him about

00:18:31.080 --> 00:18:35.440
Halfway through the movie (?) in a sequence

00:18:35.640 --> 00:18:38.720
That is completely devoid of tension

00:18:39.440 --> 00:18:45.760
Because they need to move the plot along.

00:18:48.160 --> 00:18:55.080
Okay, the middle segment of this movie is actually where the editing is is worst.

00:18:56.450 --> 00:19:00.519
So the League

00:19:01.999 --> 00:19:03.710
gets together and

00:19:03.720 --> 00:19:08.280
They have a showdown with Steppenwolf.

00:19:08.280 --> 00:19:14.040
Let's do a little bit of a plot rundown...

00:19:14.040 --> 00:19:22.440
...Steppenwolf wants three MacGuffins. He takes one from the Amazons,

00:19:22.440 --> 00:19:28.880
He takes one from the Atlanteans, and then he's looking for the third, and

00:19:29.160 --> 00:19:33.920
The League has a short little showdown with him

00:19:34.129 --> 00:19:39.789
And they managed to kinda get him to scram. He takes off

00:19:40.540 --> 00:19:49.120
and they go and grab the third MacGuffin and hold on to it and decide to use it to resurrect Superman and

00:19:49.120 --> 00:19:52.460
the time between deciding

00:19:53.000 --> 00:20:00.880
To resurrect him or to use the MacGuffin to to cure death, and

00:20:01.320 --> 00:20:09.040
Superman being just live again is about five minutes. Maybe ten.

00:20:09.840 --> 00:20:16.160
It goes very quickly with zero complications more or less

00:20:17.440 --> 00:20:24.880
And is a very silly sequence, but not in an intended silly way.

00:20:25.320 --> 00:20:33.000
It's just silly because it moves way too fast and then

00:20:34.680 --> 00:20:42.520
The big, the patchiest moment ends up happening in this where

00:20:45.020 --> 00:20:51.429
Superman comes back alive and MacGuffin goes flying into the air, and then they show it landing on top of a cop car.

00:20:51.840 --> 00:20:54.280
It's a mother box by the way.

00:20:55.000 --> 00:21:05.240
They show the mother box landing on top of a cop car and then everybody ignores it

00:21:05.280 --> 00:21:11.560
Despite the fact they know, the team knows explicitly

00:21:11.840 --> 00:21:19.800
That if Steppenwolf gets his hands on this third mother box, he will be able to destroy the world.

00:21:22.160 --> 00:21:25.480
And they know he has two.

00:21:25.520 --> 00:21:30.560
They know he has two, they've got the third, and they cannot let him get it.

00:21:32.920 --> 00:21:37.800
It lands on this cop car and they just leave it there.

00:21:37.800 --> 00:21:40.200
They just ignore him.

00:21:40.280 --> 00:21:46.960
There's Flash, Wonder Woman, Cyborg, Aquaman, Batman.

00:21:47.320 --> 00:21:48.590
Five.

00:21:48.590 --> 00:21:52.600
They got five of them, five people and all five people

00:21:53.200 --> 00:21:56.720
go and just

00:21:56.760 --> 00:21:59.920
Have this confrontation with Superman.

00:21:59.920 --> 00:22:03.040
Okay, fine with this so far.

00:22:03.040 --> 00:22:11.520
Then, in a hilarious moment, they see Steppenwolf's, like, Stargate pillar thing open his portal--

00:22:11.520 --> 00:22:13.430
[Portal opening sounds]

00:22:13.430 --> 00:22:15.430
--Opens.

00:22:15.720 --> 00:22:21.600
They cut away to the mother box lying on top of this cop car

00:22:21.600 --> 00:22:25.600
then back to the heroes and the heroes are like, "Oh, crap!

00:22:26.040 --> 00:22:28.760
The mother box! We gotta go get it"

00:22:28.760 --> 00:22:33.600
And then the portal just closes and it's like, "Oh, I guess Steppenwolf has it now

00:22:33.900 --> 00:22:35.640
We screwed up

00:22:35.640 --> 00:22:37.640
Dang"

00:22:41.940 --> 00:22:43.170
It's

00:22:43.170 --> 00:22:46.640
It's pretty funny. You don't even see

00:22:46.760 --> 00:22:54.000
You don't even see Steppenwolf in that segment, so that is probably the cludgiest editing moment where, like, I don't know

00:22:54.000 --> 00:22:56.800
what originally happened there.

00:22:58.120 --> 00:23:05.160
Everything in, not quite everything, a huge, huge portion

00:23:05.730 --> 00:23:12.919
Of that sequence is clearly reshoots, so potentially all of it is reshoots.

00:23:13.200 --> 00:23:17.240
So who knows what that's papering over.

00:23:18.000 --> 00:23:25.840
You know who knows what was originally supposed to happen in that chunk of the movie to get from here to there

00:23:25.960 --> 00:23:31.680
And the Superman reshoots are so pervasive,

00:23:32.160 --> 00:23:37.280
There are so many of them, that they're obvious, that I'm actually

00:23:38.850 --> 00:23:40.850
Kind of wondering

00:23:42.360 --> 00:23:44.360
